WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 123: Andrew Jackson and the Nullification Crisis: Standing Alone for the Union

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.840
[Rebecca] Today, we conclude our journey through the presidency of Andrew Jackson with the supreme constitutional crisis of the 1830s, the nullification crisis.

4
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:22.880
[Rebecca] Long before the Civil War, South Carolina declared federal laws void and threatened to rip the United States apart.

5
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.200
[Rebecca] Standing in the doorway of rebellion was President Andrew Jackson.

6
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:38.960
[Rebecca] Facing down his own vice president and his home state, Old Hickory proved that his deepest, most unshakable allegiance belonged to one thing alone, the perpetuity of the American Union.

7
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.080
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full story.

8
00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:06.160
[George] Designed to protect infant northern manufacturing industries from cheap British imported goods, the tariff placed duties as high as 45% on imported textiles, iron, and manufactured products.

9
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:18.720
[George] In the agricultural South, where the economy was completely dependent on exporting raw cotton to European mills and importing finished manufactured goods, the legislation was met with unbridled fury.

10
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.720
[George] Southerners bitterly denounced it as the tariff of abominations.

11
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.080
[George] Nowhere was the outrage hotter than in South Carolina.

12
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:32.160
[George] Once the wealthiest state in the South, South Carolina was suffering through a severe economic depression.

13
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:47.440
[George] Cotton prices had collapsed from 30 cents a pound in 1818 to barely nine cents a pound a decade later, while soil exhaustion in the Carolina Lowcountry drove planters to abandon their lands for the fertile black soil of Alabama and Mississippi.

14
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:59.200
[George] Rather than blaming soil depletion and overproduction, South Carolina planters blamed the federal tariff, arguing that northern capitalists were deliberately plundering the South to enrich themselves.

15
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:06.280
[George] Beneath the economic fury lay a far darker, existential terror, the defense of human slavery.

16
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:19.480
[George] South Carolina had a black majority population, and white planters were haunted by the nightmare of slave insurrections, having narrowly thwarted Denmark Vesey's massive planned uprising in Charleston in 1822.

17
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:30.440
[George] Planters reasoned that if the federal government had the constitutional power to impose devastating tariffs on Southern trade, it would one day possess the power to abolish slavery itself.

18
00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:36.440
[George] The tariff became the surrogate battleground for the survival of the Southern plantation aristocracy.

19
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:42.760
[George] Into this volatile crucible stepped the brilliant, brooding vice president of the United States, John C.

20
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:43.880
[George] Calhoun.

21
00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:50.920
[George] A native of South Carolina, Calhoun was widely regarded as the most formidable political intellect of his generation.

22
00:02:50.920 --> 00:03:01.000
[George] In the autumn of 1828, Calhoun secretly authored an explosive, anonymous pamphlet titled The South Carolina Exposition and Protestant.

23
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:10.600
[George] Calhoun revived and radicalized the states rights theories first articulated by Jefferson and Madison in the Kentucky and Virginia resolutions of 1798.

24
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:18.200
[George] Calhoun argued that the United States was not a single sovereign nation, but a compact of independent sovereign states.

25
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:28.520
[George] Because the states had created the federal government as their agent, each individual state remained the ultimate judge of whether federal laws exceeded constitutional authority.

26
00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:40.920
[George] Therefore, Calhoun concluded, if a state determined that a federal law violated the Constitution, a special state convention had the sovereign right to declare that law null and void within its borders.

27
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:50.800
[George] Nullification, Calhoun argued, was not secession or rebellion, but a peaceful constitutional veto that would protect the minority from the tyranny of the majority.

28
00:03:51.040 --> 00:04:01.360
[George] If the federal government refused to accept nullification, Calhoun argued, the state retained the ultimate right of secession, peacefully withdrawing from the Union.

29
00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:08.240
[George] Calhoun kept his authorship secret, waiting for Andrew Jackson to take office as president in 1829.

30
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:15.440
[George] Calhoun assumed that Jackson, a fellow Southerner, cotton planter and slaveholder, would embrace the doctrine.

31
00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.680
[George] It was the most catastrophic miscalculation of Calhoun's life.

32
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:26.320
[George] For more than a year, Andrew Jackson watched the gathering nullification storm in cold, watchful silence.

33
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.800
[George] In January 1830, the United States Senate exploded into the legendary Webster Hayne debate.

34
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:47.680
[George] Where South Carolina Senator Robert Hain championed nullification and Daniel Webster of Massachusetts delivered his immortal defense of the nation, concluding with the thunderous words, Liberty and Union, now and forever, one and inseparable.

35
00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.000
[George] Jackson made no public comment.

36
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.040
[George] But in private, his fury was boiling.

37
00:04:53.040 --> 00:05:03.760
[George] The showdown occurred on April 13, 1830, at the annual Democratic banquet celebrating Thomas Jefferson's birthday at Brown's Indian Queen Hotel in Washington.

38
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:13.280
[George] Calhoun and his Southern allies had organized the dinner, intending to use Jefferson's memory to publicly bind the Democratic Party to the doctrine of nullification.

39
00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:20.640
[George] The printed program featured twenty-four elaborate toasts, all praising states' rights and warning against federal tyranny.

40
00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:27.680
[George] When the regular toasts were concluded, President Andrew Jackson was invited to give the first volunteer toast of the evening.

41
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:37.880
[George] The banquet hall fell dead silent as the 63-year-old president stood up, looking straight across the table into the piercing eyes of his vice president, John C.

42
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:44.200
[George] Calhoun, Jackson raised his glass high and delivered a toast that shook the room like an earthquake.

43
00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.240
[George] Our federal union, it must be preserved.

44
00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.920
[George] The effect was electric.

45
00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.400
[George] Calhoun turned pale as a sheet.

46
00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:56.680
[George] Trembling, the vice president rose to give his response, his voice shaking.

47
00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:00.280
[George] The Union, next to our liberty, the most dear.

48
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:08.360
[George] May we all remember that it can only be preserved by respecting the rights of the states and distributing equally the benefit and burden of the Union.

49
00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:11.160
[George] The battle lines were irrevocably drawn.

50
00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.520
[George] Jackson had served notice, anyone who threatened the Union would answer to old hickory.

51
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:24.760
[George] In the summer of 1832, Congress attempted to defuse the crisis by passing a new tariff bill that lowered duties significantly.

52
00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:30.680
[George] But for South Carolina radicals, known as the Nullifiers, compromise came too late.

53
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:43.320
[George] In October, nullifiers swept the state legislative elections, and on November 24, 1832, a special state convention in Columbia passed the fateful ordinance of nullification.

54
00:06:43.320 --> 00:07:01.960
[George] The ordinance declared the Federal Tariff Acts of 1828 and 1832, unauthorized by the Constitution of the United States, and violate of the true meaning and intent thereof, and are null, void, and no law, nor binding upon this state, its officers, or its citizens.

55
00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:21.680
[George] The convention forbade any appeal of state court rulings to the United States Supreme Court, made it illegal for federal customs officials to collect duties within South Carolina after February 1, 1833, and required all state officials and military officers to take an oath of loyalty to the nullification ordinance.

56
00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:40.800
[George] Most ominously, South Carolina declared that any attempt by the federal government to use force, blockade its harbors, or enforce the tariff would be regarded as an act of war, releasing the citizens of South Carolina from all political connection with the United States and plunging the state into immediate secession.

57
00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:50.960
[George] Governor Robert Hain called for 25,000 volunteer militia to arm themselves with muskets and blue cockades, preparing to repel any federal invasion.

58
00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.000
[George] John C.

59
00:07:52.320 --> 00:08:00.400
[George] Calhoun resigned the vice presidency to take a vacant seat in the Senate, returning to Washington to lead the political defense of his state.

60
00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:08.720
[George] When the news of South Carolina's ordinance reached the White House, Andrew Jackson exploded in righteous, uncompromising fury.

61
00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.120
[George] To Jackson, nullification was not an abstract legal theory.

62
00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:21.840
[George] It was treason, an armed insurrection against the sovereign will of the American people that would reduce the United States to a laughingstock among nations.

63
00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:30.960
[George] Jackson slammed his fist onto his desk and declared to his cabinet Tell them that they can talk and write resolutions and print threats to their hearts content.

64
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:40.720
[George] But if one drop of blood be shed there in defiance of the laws of the United States, I will hang the first man of them I can get my hands on to the first tree I can find.

65
00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:51.840
[George] On December 10, 1832, Jackson issued his historic proclamation to the people of South Carolina, drafted with the assistance of Secretary of State Edward Livingston.

66
00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.840
[George] It remains one of the greatest state papers in American history.

67
00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.000
[George] Jackson dismantled Calhoun's constitutional arguments with devastating clarity.

68
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:06.360
[George] He wrote, The Constitution of the United States forms a government, not a league.

69
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:12.920
[George] To say that any state may at pleasure secede from the Union is to say that the United States is not a nation.

70
00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:19.880
[George] Jackson addressed the citizens of his native state not with hatred, but with fatherly solemnity and stern resolve.

71
00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:23.000
[George] The laws of the United States must be executed.

72
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.880
[George] I have no discretionary power on the subject.

73
00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.400
[George] My duty is emphatically pronounced in the Constitution.

74
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:33.880
[George] Those who told you that you might peaceably prevent their execution have deceived you.

75
00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:38.040
[George] Their object is disunion, but be not deceived by names.

76
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.760
[George] Disunion by armed force is treason.

77
00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.000
[George] Are you ready to incur its guilt?

78
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:48.840
[George] The proclamation rallied Northern and Western states in an overwhelming wave of Unionist fervor.

79
00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.200
[George] Jackson was not a man who issued empty warnings.

80
00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.000
[George] While issuing his proclamation, he prepared for immediate military action.

81
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:06.840
[George] He dispatched General Winfield Scott to Charleston to take command of Federal forces, heavily reinforcing Fort Moultrie and Castle Pinckney in Charleston Harbor.

82
00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:17.880
[George] He ordered two naval revenue cutters and the heavy sloop of war USS Natchez into Charleston waters, with orders to collect federal duties offshore from any arriving merchant vessels.

83
00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:34.040
[George] In January 1833, Jackson sent a special message to Congress requesting what became known as the Force Bill, explicit legislation authorizing the President to use the United States Army and Navy to enforce federal revenue laws and suppress rebellion.

84
00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:45.520
[George] Jackson quietly prepared 40,000 federal troops, informing his generals that he was prepared to personally take the field as Commander-in-Chief and march on South Carolina at a moment's notice.

85
00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:49.120
[George] South Carolina suddenly found itself completely isolated.

86
00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:56.960
[George] The nullifiers had assumed that other Southern states like Virginia, Georgia, and North Carolina would rally to their defense.

87
00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:05.920
[George] Instead, every single Southern state legislature passed resolutions condemning nullification as dangerous, reckless, and unconstitutional.

88
00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:11.120
[George] South Carolina stood alone against an enraged nation and an immovable president.

89
00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:21.680
[George] Recognizing that armed conflict would destroy the South, Senator Henry Clay of Kentucky, the great compromiser, stepped into the breach, working secretly with John C.

90
00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:32.240
[George] Calhoun, Clay drafted the Compromise Tariff of 1833, which provided for a gradual, systematic reduction of tariff rates over a ten-year period.

91
00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:44.000
[George] On March 2, 1833, Jackson signed both bills into law simultaneously, the force bill providing the sword and the compromise tariff providing the olive branch.

92
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:53.200
[George] Confronted with Jackson's overwhelming military preparations and given an honorable retreat through Clay's compromise tariff, South Carolina blinked.

93
00:11:53.200 --> 00:12:01.760
[George] On March 11, 1833, the South Carolina Convention reconvened and voted to rescind its ordinance of nullification.

94
00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:14.400
[George] In a final, petulant gesture of defiance, the convention voted to nullify the Force Bill, which Jackson treated with amused indifference, as the force bill was now a dead letter with the tariff peace established.

95
00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:20.800
[George] The nullification crisis was a monumental victory for Andrew Jackson and the supremacy of the Union.

96
00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:32.840
[George] By demonstrating that the federal government possessed both the constitutional authority and the military will to crush state rebellion, Jackson preserved the American Republic 30 years before the Civil War.

97
00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:42.040
[George] He proved that the Constitution was an enduring framework for a sovereign nation, not a voluntary partnership to be dissolved at the whim of disgruntled factions.

98
00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:46.840
[George] Yet Jackson harbored no illusions about the true motives of the Southern radicals.

99
00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:54.280
[George] Writing to his close friend Martin Van Buren in May 1833, Jackson issued a chilling, prophetic warning.

100
00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:03.880
[George] The next pretext will be the Negro or slavery question.

101
00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:06.360
[George] History would prove old hickory right.

102
00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:15.320
[George] Twenty-eight years later, South Carolina would indeed lead the South into secession over slavery, triggering four years of catastrophic civil war.

103
00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:26.440
[George] But in 1832, when the Republic stood on the edge of the abyss, Andrew Jackson stood like a granite fortress in the breach, proving that the Union could and would be preserved.

104
00:13:46.760 --> 00:14:02.600
[Rebecca] Next week, we meet Jackson's chosen successor, America's eighth president, Martin Van Buren, the brilliant, masterly political operator known as the Little Magician and the Red Fox of Kinderhook, who created the modern American two party system.

105
00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:06.840
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

